WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.834 --> 00:00:02.133
-Our next guest is

00:00:02.133 --> 00:00:04.067
an Academy Award-winning
producer, director, and actor.

00:00:04.067 --> 00:00:06.901
His documentary series "Beckham"
is streaming now on Netflix.

00:00:06.901 --> 00:00:08.167
Let's take a look.

00:00:08.167 --> 00:00:10.133
-You know, playing
at the national team stadium,

00:00:10.133 --> 00:00:12.634
playing at Wembley...

00:00:12.634 --> 00:00:16.267
it's a very emotional
moment for me,

00:00:16.267 --> 00:00:18.701
because my dad always used to
take me to Wembley

00:00:18.701 --> 00:00:20.300
to watch England play.

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:23.767
That was really what
my childhood was all about.

00:00:27.601 --> 00:00:31.701
-So seeing my son come out
for the first time at Wembley,

00:00:31.701 --> 00:00:35.434
I must admit, I had a --
I had a little tear.

00:00:35.434 --> 00:00:38.634
-Please welcome to the show
Fisher Stevens, everybody.

00:00:38.634 --> 00:00:40.634
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:40.634 --> 00:00:47.534
♪♪

00:00:47.534 --> 00:00:54.467
♪♪

00:00:54.467 --> 00:00:56.100
Welcome to the show, Fisher.

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:58.834
-Thank you.
So great to be here.

00:00:58.834 --> 00:01:00.467
-I'm so happy to have you.

00:01:00.467 --> 00:01:02.267
I know you are
a proud member of SAG,

00:01:02.267 --> 00:01:04.567
and to respect
the rules of the strike,

00:01:04.567 --> 00:01:06.901
we're not going to talk about
any of your acting work,

00:01:06.901 --> 00:01:09.000
but I am just going to
vaguely say

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:12.067
you were in an excellent show
that ended this year,

00:01:12.067 --> 00:01:13.701
and you were excellent in it.

00:01:13.701 --> 00:01:15.801
-Thank you.
-Yes. Keep it vague.

00:01:15.801 --> 00:01:18.300
-Thank you.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:20.567
I loved being on that show

00:01:20.567 --> 00:01:23.067
and doing that show
and sad it ended.

00:01:23.067 --> 00:01:24.601
-There we go.
I think that's perfect.

00:01:24.601 --> 00:01:25.901
That show.

00:01:25.901 --> 00:01:29.801
You are an Oscar-winning
documentary filmmaker,

00:01:29.801 --> 00:01:31.834
and this came across your plate,

00:01:31.834 --> 00:01:34.334
this chance to do a documentary
about David Beckham.

00:01:34.334 --> 00:01:36.667
You weren't necessarily
a giant fan of his.

00:01:36.667 --> 00:01:37.801
This wasn't the sort of project

00:01:37.801 --> 00:01:39.400
you were out there
trying to land.

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:43.033
-Right, I actually --
I got a call

00:01:43.033 --> 00:01:45.868
on my way to that show, to work,

00:01:45.868 --> 00:01:50.868
and it was from my old pal
Leonardo DiCaprio's office

00:01:50.868 --> 00:01:52.067
saying --

00:01:52.067 --> 00:01:54.834
-What a funny way
to end that sentence.

00:01:54.834 --> 00:01:57.634
"My dear friend
Leo DiCaprio's office."

00:01:57.634 --> 00:02:00.701
-We had made -- We made
some documentaries together,

00:02:00.701 --> 00:02:04.400
and the office said,
"Leo suggested you

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:06.434
to direct David Beckham's
documentary,

00:02:06.434 --> 00:02:07.634
and David wants to talk to you."

00:02:07.634 --> 00:02:10.567
And I was like, "Me?
David Beckham?"

00:02:10.567 --> 00:02:13.000
I really wasn't that excited.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:14.667
I love football, soccer.

00:02:14.667 --> 00:02:19.033
I really got into it after he
left England and went to Madrid

00:02:19.033 --> 00:02:22.000
and just thought,
Brand Beckham, Posh Spice,

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:23.801
wasn't really that interested.

00:02:23.801 --> 00:02:25.868
And then I went to work
on that show,

00:02:25.868 --> 00:02:28.501
and the people that worked on it
are all English,

00:02:28.501 --> 00:02:29.834
and they're like, "You have to.

00:02:29.834 --> 00:02:31.701
He's a legend.
She's amazing.

00:02:31.701 --> 00:02:33.601
You don't know
what the guy went through.

00:02:33.601 --> 00:02:34.801
Great story."

00:02:34.801 --> 00:02:37.100
And I did a Zoom with him,

00:02:37.100 --> 00:02:38.634
and he was very different
than I thought,

00:02:38.634 --> 00:02:41.167
and then met him
and his wife for dinner,

00:02:41.167 --> 00:02:43.334
and they were amazing, much --

00:02:43.334 --> 00:02:46.067
We had a few drinks,
so it was very loose,

00:02:46.067 --> 00:02:50.467
and they said, "Oh, Fisher,
you don't know our story.

00:02:50.467 --> 00:02:52.200
You don't know our story."

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:55.834
And I got excited,
and that was it, that was it is.

00:02:55.834 --> 00:03:00.234
-You know, being a football fan
and especially that era,

00:03:00.234 --> 00:03:02.434
that World Cup that sort of ends
the first episode,

00:03:02.434 --> 00:03:05.234
that's when I really feel like
I fell in love with the sport.

00:03:05.234 --> 00:03:06.467
So I was so excited for that.

00:03:06.467 --> 00:03:09.200
This is a love story,
though, man.

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:11.601
It is so wonderful
to watch this couple

00:03:11.601 --> 00:03:13.200
and the way they talk
about each other

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:15.100
and the way they open up to you.

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:16.968
Do you feel like
it was easy for them,

00:03:16.968 --> 00:03:19.501
or were they hesitant at all?

00:03:19.501 --> 00:03:21.133
-Well, I asked them --

00:03:21.133 --> 00:03:23.033
I'm like, "Why do you
want to do this, man?

00:03:23.033 --> 00:03:24.501
You know, I'm going to be
bothering you

00:03:24.501 --> 00:03:26.067
for the next year and a half."

00:03:26.067 --> 00:03:28.601
I need hours and hours,
especially --

00:03:28.601 --> 00:03:31.701
You know, David is not a guy
that's used to sitting

00:03:31.701 --> 00:03:33.434
and talking
for more than 15 minutes.

00:03:33.434 --> 00:03:35.367
He's used to, "Let's go.
I got to go here.

00:03:35.367 --> 00:03:37.100
I got to promote sunglasses,
then whiskey,

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:38.634
then this and that."

00:03:38.634 --> 00:03:40.734
And I said, "No, we're
going to do three hours,

00:03:40.734 --> 00:03:42.234
and I need time."

00:03:42.234 --> 00:03:44.801
And he was willing.
He really was.

00:03:44.801 --> 00:03:46.234
But it took, you know, hours.

00:03:46.234 --> 00:03:47.534
I was with David for over,
you know,

00:03:47.534 --> 00:03:49.701
probably about 40-plus hours.

00:03:49.701 --> 00:03:51.400
Victoria was much more
used to it.

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:53.000
She's like, "Let's go.
I want to talk about this.

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:55.200
I want to get it off my chest."

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:57.267
And she was wonderful.

00:03:57.267 --> 00:04:01.567
But, yeah, I mean, look,
it's hard to go back

00:04:01.567 --> 00:04:03.367
and think about --
I mean, all of you out there,

00:04:03.367 --> 00:04:06.000
you, Seth, I say, "Remember
that time in your life,"

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:08.501
you don't want to remember,
and you start talking about it.

00:04:08.501 --> 00:04:13.067
It's not always easy, but
I'm glad -- I'm glad he did.

00:04:13.067 --> 00:04:14.400
-And, you know,
it's interesting you say

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:16.334
it's not always easy,
because I think sometimes

00:04:16.334 --> 00:04:19.133
when someone's been around
as long as they have,

00:04:19.133 --> 00:04:22.968
you only think of all
the good fortune they've had.

00:04:22.968 --> 00:04:24.400
And this documentary shows

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:26.267
that there were these
massive highs and lows.

00:04:26.267 --> 00:04:27.667
And it covers them,

00:04:27.667 --> 00:04:29.334
and you sort of live
through them with him.

00:04:29.334 --> 00:04:31.067
There are also great,
really funny,

00:04:31.067 --> 00:04:33.234
really human mov-- moments,
excuse me,

00:04:33.234 --> 00:04:35.000
that as a filmmaker,
you must think,

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:38.133
"I can't believe
I'm catching this moment."

00:04:38.133 --> 00:04:40.100
And I want to show a clip,
but can you just --

00:04:40.100 --> 00:04:41.467
Did you know how good this was

00:04:41.467 --> 00:04:43.834
when Victoria was talking
about her upbringing

00:04:43.834 --> 00:04:47.067
and David popped his head in?

00:04:47.067 --> 00:04:49.734
-Well, I was kind of pissed off,
because I was like,

00:04:49.734 --> 00:04:52.300
"What is he doing here?"
-Yeah.

00:04:52.300 --> 00:04:55.267
-I said, "She's not there when
you're doing your interviews."

00:04:55.267 --> 00:04:57.834
So -- But I knew
it was kind of magical.

00:04:57.834 --> 00:04:59.501
And my cameraman and I
work together so much.

00:04:59.501 --> 00:05:01.367
He just like --
You'll see. They just --

00:05:01.367 --> 00:05:02.901
We flip the camera on him.

00:05:02.901 --> 00:05:05.601
But then I told him he's got to
get out of here, man, 'cause --

00:05:05.601 --> 00:05:07.634
-By the way, you know
why he had to get out of there?

00:05:07.634 --> 00:05:10.901
'Cause he dropped the mic
and it was so good.

00:05:10.901 --> 00:05:12.501
Let's take a look
at this wonderful clip

00:05:12.501 --> 00:05:13.601
between a married couple.

00:05:13.601 --> 00:05:16.033
-Both of our parents
work really hard.

00:05:16.033 --> 00:05:18.434
We're very working-class.

00:05:18.434 --> 00:05:19.734
-Be honest.

00:05:19.734 --> 00:05:21.868
-I am being honest.
-Be honest.

00:05:21.868 --> 00:05:24.367
-I am being honest.
-What car did your dad

00:05:24.367 --> 00:05:26.000
drive you to school in?

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:28.067
-So, my dad --
-No, one answer.

00:05:28.067 --> 00:05:29.467
-My dad --
-What car was it?

00:05:29.467 --> 00:05:31.167
-It's not a simple answer,
because --

00:05:31.167 --> 00:05:34.167
-What car did you get your dad
to drive you to school in?

00:05:34.167 --> 00:05:36.033
-It depends.
-No, no, no, no, no.

00:05:36.033 --> 00:05:38.634
-Okay, in the '80s,
my dad had a Rolls-Royce.

00:05:38.634 --> 00:05:39.868
-Thank you.

00:05:39.868 --> 00:05:41.501
[ Laughter ]

00:05:41.501 --> 00:05:44.367
[ Applause ]

00:05:44.367 --> 00:05:46.467
-So good.
-I know.

00:05:46.467 --> 00:05:48.133
-You also -- I mean,

00:05:48.133 --> 00:05:50.300
you interview, you know,
Sir Alex Ferguson.

00:05:50.300 --> 00:05:51.968
You interview his old teammates.

00:05:51.968 --> 00:05:56.000
You interview opponents,
former paparazzi.

00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:57.300
Most of them just have nothing

00:05:57.300 --> 00:05:59.300
but wonderful things
to say about him.

00:05:59.300 --> 00:06:02.467
And it's really -- I really
enjoy his former teammates,

00:06:02.467 --> 00:06:05.734
because they are -- a lot of
them are giving him a hard time,

00:06:05.734 --> 00:06:08.200
and they're making fun of how
much money he spent on clothes,

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:10.901
and the fact that, you know,
he spent a lot of money

00:06:10.901 --> 00:06:12.400
to buy a pen
really made me laugh.

00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:15.200
But you can tell
they just love him so much.

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:18.133
-He's a lovable character,
I have to say.

00:06:18.133 --> 00:06:21.501
I did try to get people
to talk [bleep] about him,

00:06:21.501 --> 00:06:24.467
and nobody would.

00:06:24.467 --> 00:06:29.100
I guess Glenn Hoddle, who was
his former manager of England,

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:31.901
he may have,
but he wouldn't talk.

00:06:31.901 --> 00:06:34.834
I tried to get Roy Keane,
who's a bruiser.

00:06:34.834 --> 00:06:37.267
He's very famous.
He was the captain.

00:06:37.267 --> 00:06:39.701
I said, "Roy, when he showed up,
you know,

00:06:39.701 --> 00:06:41.000
with these haircuts
and wearing --

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:42.667
and when he was in a sarong,

00:06:42.667 --> 00:06:44.834
weren't you, like, upset
that he was, like, taking --"

00:06:44.834 --> 00:06:46.934
He said, "I loved him,
I loved him."

00:06:46.934 --> 00:06:50.300
I'm like, geez, so I couldn't
get anybody to talk --

00:06:50.300 --> 00:06:52.801
Yeah, but he's a lovable guy.

00:06:52.801 --> 00:06:54.400
-It's interesting
because I wonder

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:58.067
if you found,
in doing this exercise,

00:06:58.067 --> 00:07:00.701
there's these wonderful shots
where you are filming him

00:07:00.701 --> 00:07:02.367
and other players
watching themselves.

00:07:02.367 --> 00:07:04.601
-Yeah.
-And you get the sense

00:07:04.601 --> 00:07:06.133
that these athletes --

00:07:06.133 --> 00:07:08.801
and obviously,
I guess having lived it,

00:07:08.801 --> 00:07:12.167
they don't feel much need to go
back and watch old highlights.

00:07:12.167 --> 00:07:14.033
-Yeah.
-And so you not only realize,

00:07:14.033 --> 00:07:16.133
"Oh, I'm watching him
watch this play,"

00:07:16.133 --> 00:07:18.133
but he maybe hasn't watched it
since it happened.

00:07:18.133 --> 00:07:21.367
-Yeah, with the exception of the
Greece -- famous Greece goal,

00:07:21.367 --> 00:07:24.701
which he had watched recently,
he hadn't seen himself play.

00:07:24.701 --> 00:07:26.667
I showed him --

00:07:26.667 --> 00:07:29.067
There's a clip where I show him
his first appearance.

00:07:29.067 --> 00:07:30.601
He's 17 years old.

00:07:30.601 --> 00:07:33.834
He comes out like a little fawn,
like he could barely walk.

00:07:33.834 --> 00:07:36.200
And he hadn't seen that --

00:07:36.200 --> 00:07:38.334
I don't think he'd ever
seen that or -- you know?

00:07:38.334 --> 00:07:40.300
And it blew his mind, right?

00:07:40.300 --> 00:07:42.567
And he's so vulnerable
and he's looking at it

00:07:42.567 --> 00:07:44.734
and he's, like,
getting emotional.

00:07:44.734 --> 00:07:49.267
And Eric Cantona,
a legendary French player,

00:07:49.267 --> 00:07:53.467
hadn't obviously watched him
play -- himself play for years.

00:07:53.467 --> 00:07:56.234
And just to get those --
those expressions,

00:07:56.234 --> 00:07:58.234
that vulnerability
of these tough,

00:07:58.234 --> 00:08:01.434
hard athletes being
like little children again

00:08:01.434 --> 00:08:03.868
was sort of what we were after,
and especially David,

00:08:03.868 --> 00:08:07.334
because just to see into
his soul for that brief moment

00:08:07.334 --> 00:08:08.634
was sort of what we were after.

00:08:08.634 --> 00:08:09.834
-There's a real wonderful moment

00:08:09.834 --> 00:08:11.734
because, again,
I think of Eric Cantona

00:08:11.734 --> 00:08:14.300
as this very
sort of hard player.

00:08:14.300 --> 00:08:17.133
This guy did not
show much emotion

00:08:17.133 --> 00:08:19.200
other than,
it seemed like, rage.

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:20.501
-Yes.
Yeah.

00:08:20.501 --> 00:08:23.334
-And you tell him --
'cause you show that clip

00:08:23.334 --> 00:08:24.601
from the first game
and you say, you know,

00:08:24.601 --> 00:08:26.501
"David said the best moment
about his first goal

00:08:26.501 --> 00:08:28.033
was he got to run over
and hug you,"

00:08:28.033 --> 00:08:31.033
and you watch it land
on Eric Cantona.

00:08:31.033 --> 00:08:32.634
And he just goes, "He did?"
-Yeah.

00:08:32.634 --> 00:08:34.934
-And it's so --
it's a really wonderful moment.

00:08:34.934 --> 00:08:36.534
-Yeah.
-I want to finish

00:08:36.534 --> 00:08:38.434
because, you know, we started
by talking about SAG.

00:08:38.434 --> 00:08:40.434
I did not realize this
until today.

00:08:40.434 --> 00:08:43.167
You were not born
Fisher Stevens.

00:08:43.167 --> 00:08:45.167
And SAG is the reason
you are Fisher Stevens.

00:08:45.167 --> 00:08:47.868
-Right, my name --
I was born Mike Johnson.

00:08:47.868 --> 00:08:49.300
[ Laughter ]

00:08:49.300 --> 00:08:50.300
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:50.300 --> 00:08:52.434
And it was like...

00:08:52.434 --> 00:08:55.634
There was a guy named
Mike Johnson.

00:08:55.634 --> 00:08:57.834
-Guess what.
You just did your Rolls-Royce.

00:08:57.834 --> 00:08:59.667
That's real good.

00:08:59.667 --> 00:09:02.968
-No, no,
I was born Steven Fisher,

00:09:02.968 --> 00:09:06.133
and when I got my SAG card,

00:09:06.133 --> 00:09:08.868
and the producers of the movie
that I had just done called me

00:09:08.868 --> 00:09:11.133
and said, "You can't use
your name, Steven Fisher.

00:09:11.133 --> 00:09:13.067
You need to either
put a middle name in there

00:09:13.067 --> 00:09:14.601
or change your name."

00:09:14.601 --> 00:09:17.534
And everybody called me "Fish."
My dad called me Fish.

00:09:17.534 --> 00:09:19.167
And there was a sign
that I used to see.

00:09:19.167 --> 00:09:24.367
It said Fisher-Stevens Stoves
in Brooklyn on this building.

00:09:24.367 --> 00:09:26.968
And I got the idea, I'll just
reverse it -- Fisher Stevens.

00:09:26.968 --> 00:09:30.934
But I changed the Stephen
from S-T-E-P-H-E-N

00:09:30.934 --> 00:09:32.133
to S-T-E-V-E-N-S.

00:09:32.133 --> 00:09:35.300
But, yeah, so let's get back
to work, SAG.

00:09:35.300 --> 00:09:36.567
Come on!
-Let's go.

00:09:36.567 --> 00:09:39.534
We got Fisher Stevens
who's chomping at the bit.

00:09:39.534 --> 00:09:41.434
"Beckham" is streaming now
on Netflix.

00:09:41.434 --> 00:09:42.634
It's fantastic.

00:09:42.634 --> 00:09:44.300
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

